{
  "id": "check-factorising-single-brackets-2",
  "title": "Check: Factorising Single Brackets",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise fully $12ab + 18a$.",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "$6(2ab + 3a)$" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "$6a(2b + 3)$" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "$2a(6b + 9)$" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "$3a(4b + 6)$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "The HCF of $12ab$ and $18a$ is $6a$. Dividing: $12ab \\div 6a = 2b$ and $18a \\div 6a = 3$, giving $6a(2b + 3)$. This is fully factorised because $2b + 3$ has no common factors."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Factorise $x^3 + x^2$.",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "$x(x^2 + x)$" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "$x^2(x + 1)$" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "$x^3(1 + x)$" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "$x^2(x + x)$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "The HCF of $x^3$ and $x^2$ is $x^2$. Dividing: $x^3 \\div x^2 = x$ and $x^2 \\div x^2 = 1$, giving $x^2(x + 1)$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Which expression is not fully factorised?",
      "options": [
        { "id": "a", "text": "$3(x + 2)$" },
        { "id": "b", "text": "$5x(2y - 1)$" },
        { "id": "c", "text": "$4(2x + 6)$" },
        { "id": "d", "text": "$7a(b + 3)$" }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "$4(2x + 6)$ is not fully factorised because $2x + 6$ still has a common factor of $2$. The fully factorised form is $8(x + 3)$."
    }
  ]
}
